#### Scenario 2: Technical Debt and Sprint Commitments

**Question:** "Your team is committing to 25 points every sprint, but 
technical debt is growing (test coverage dropping, code complexity increasing). 
How do you balance business features with technical health?"

**Strategic answer:**
```
Problem analysis:
The tension:
├─ Business wants features shipped (25 points)
├─ Technical debt accumulating (warning signs)
├─ Quality metrics declining (red flags)
└─ Team slowing down (warning signs)

Root cause: 100% of capacity on features, 0% on tech debt

In JIRA, I'd show:
├─ Burndown: Stable at 25 points (looks good)
├─ Code coverage trend: Dropping 2-3% per sprint (bad)
├─ Cycle time: Increasing from 8 days to 12 days (bad)
├─ Velocity variance: Increasing (indicating fragility)
└─ Tech debt issues: 47 open, none being addressed

Reality:
"It looks like we're delivering, but we're actually
getting slower. Technical debt is interest on a loan."

Solution approach:

Phase 1: Transparency (Week 1)
└─ Create tech debt epic in JIRA
   Sub-tasks:
   ├─ TECH-DEBT-100: Increase test coverage (5 pts)
   ├─ TECH-DEBT-101: Refactor auth module (8 pts)
   ├─ TECH-DEBT-102: Update dependencies (3 pts)
   └─ TECH-DEBT-103: Database optimization (5 pts)

   Show team: We have 21 points of tech debt
   Current velocity: 25 points
   Real capacity: 25 - 5 = 20 points sustainable

   JIRA dashboard showing:
   ├─ Feature velocity trend ↓
   ├─ Tech debt issues ↑
   ├─ Code coverage ↓
   └─ Cycle time ↑

Phase 2: Commitment (Week 2-3)
└─ Sprint planning adjustment
   
   Proposed split:
   Before:  Features: 25 pts, Tech debt: 0 pts
   After:   Features: 18 pts, Tech debt: 5 pts
   
   Why this ratio?
   ├─ 20% tech debt is industry standard for sustainable pace
   ├─ Prevents spiral where debt compounds
   ├─ Demonstrates commitment to quality
   └─ Team feels better (less pressure = better code)

   Get agreement:
   ├─ Tech lead: "5 points will address our biggest pain points"
   ├─ Product manager: "We can ship 18 features and still hit roadmap"
   ├─ Team: "We'll have time to improve quality"
   └─ Management: "This prevents future slowdown"

Phase 3: Implementation (Ongoing)
└─ Sprint planning enforces split
   
   In JIRA:
   ├─ Create filter: "Tech Debt" with label
   ├─ In planning, select features first (18 pts)
   ├─ Then select tech debt (5 pts)
   ├─ Total doesn't exceed 23 pts (buffer)
   
   In board:
   ├─ Separate section for tech debt stories
   ├─ Visual reminder they're not features
   ├─ Track them same as features
   └─ Include in burndown and velocity

Phase 4: Monitor Progress (Weekly)
└─ Track metrics in dashboard
   
   Watch these indicators:
   ├─ Code coverage: Should increase from -2% to +1% per sprint
   ├─ Cycle time: Should decrease back to 8 days
   ├─ Velocity variance: Should stabilize
   ├─ Unresolved tech debt: Should decrease
   └─ Team sentiment: Should improve

Phase 5: Adjust Based on Results (Sprint 2-4)
└─ If metrics improving:
   ├─ Stay the course for 2-3 more sprints
   ├─ Gradually reduce tech debt % as debt decreases
   ├─ Return to 20% tech debt allocation ongoing
   
   If metrics not improving:
   ├─ Increase tech debt allocation to 7-8 points
   ├─ Root cause analysis (is selection wrong?)
   ├─ Check task dependencies (are items blocking each other?)

Expected timeline:
Sprint 1: Stabilization (velocity still 20-21)
Sprint 2-3: Quality improvement (code coverage +2-3%)
Sprint 4: Cycle time improvement (8 days target)
Sprint 5+: Sustainable pace (20-22 points with 80% features/20% debt)

JIRA visibility:
├─ Epic: "Technical Debt Reduction"
│  Progress: 8/21 tasks completed
│  Trend: On track
│
├─ Dashboard showing:
│  ├─ Tech debt velocity: 5 pts/sprint
│  ├─ Feature velocity: 18 pts/sprint
│  ├─ Code quality metrics
│  └─ Cycle time trend
│
└─ Weekly update comment:
   "Sprint 1 summary: Addressed test coverage gap in auth module.
    Code coverage improved 2%. Cycle time stable. On track."
```

**Why this answer works:**
- Shows you understand balance isn't one-time, it's ongoing
- Demonstrates data-driven decision making
- Shows business acumen (explaining to non-technical)
- Explains JIRA's role in visibility and accountability

