#### Scenario 3: Cross-Team Coordination

**Question:** "You have 3 teams (Frontend, Backend, Mobile) working on 
the same product. A frontend story depends on a backend API that's 
incomplete. How do you manage this in JIRA?"

**Systematic answer:**
```
The challenge:
Frontend can't start:  Backend still designing API
But they're blocked:   Timeline slipping
And isolated:          No visibility into each other's work

Solution framework:

Step 1: Structure JIRA for cross-team visibility
├─ Projects:
│  ├─ FRONTEND: Frontend-specific issues
│  ├─ BACKEND: Backend-specific issues
│  ├─ MOBILE: Mobile-specific issues
│  └─ API: Shared API contract definition
│
├─ Shared backlog:
│  All teams see: Product requirements (by feature)
│  Each team owns: Implementation details
│
└─ Epic linking:
   User Login Epic
   ├─ FRONTEND-201: Login UI implementation
   ├─ BACKEND-301: Login endpoint development
   ├─ MOBILE-101: Login screen iOS/Android
   └─ API-401: Login API contract specification

Step 2: Clear dependency definition
├─ Frontend story PROJ-201:
│  Linked issue: "depends on" BACKEND-301
│  Blocker: "needs API spec from API-401"
│
├─ Backend story PROJ-301:
│  Linked issue: "used by" FRONTEND-201
│  Blocker: "needs API design approved in API-401"
│
├─ API story PROJ-401:
│  Purpose: Define contract both teams agree on
│  Acceptance criteria:
│     ☐ Endpoint path defined (/api/auth/login)
│     ☐ Request format specified (POST, JSON)
│     ☐ Response format specified (JWT token, user object)
│     ☐ Error cases documented (invalid credentials, rate limit)
│     ☐ Frontend team sign-off (APPROVED)
│     ☐ Backend team sign-off (APPROVED)
│
└─ Key: API spec COMES FIRST, blocks others

Step 3: Timeline management
├─ Sprint 1:
│  ├─ API team: Design and document API (3 days)
│  ├─ Frontend: Mock API, build UI (3 days)
│  ├─ Backend: Build endpoint (5 days)
│  └─ Result: Frontend done, Backend in progress
│
├─ Sprint 2:
│  ├─ Backend: Finish endpoint, integrate with frontend
│  ├─ Mobile: Start using API (after backend complete)
│  └─ Frontend: Polish UI with real API
│
└─ Sprint 3:
   All teams: Testing together, bug fixes, deployment

Step 4: JIRA tracking of dependencies
├─ API spec story: MUST be done early (Day 3 of sprint)
│  Owner: Senior backend engineer (design-focused)
│  Review: Frontend lead + Backend lead sign off
│
├─ Frontend story: Can start on Day 4 (mock implementation)
│  Dependencies:
│  - Blocked by: API-401 (removed when API-401 DONE)
│  - Related to: BACKEND-301 (for coordination)
│
├─ Backend story: Can start Day 4 (real implementation)
│  Dependencies:
│  - Blocked by: API-401 (removed when API-401 DONE)
│  - Used by: FRONTEND-201, MOBILE-101 (visibility)
│
└─ Mobile story: Can start only when BACKEND-301 DONE
   (No need to start early if dependent)

Step 5: Workflow for cross-team coordination
├─ Status: "In Progress" means developer actively working
├─ Status: "In Review" means ready for other team to validate
├─ Status: "Blocked" means waiting on another team
│
└─ Notifications:
   When Frontend moves to "In Review":
   ├─ Notify Backend: "Frontend ready to test your API"
   ├─ Notify Mobile: "Frontend complete, coming soon to mobile"
   └─ Move feature epic to "Testing" phase

Step 6: Regular synchronization
├─ Daily:
│  ├─ Team updates status in JIRA standup
│  ├─ Blockers visible immediately
│  └─ No surprises
│
├─ Weekly (30 min):
│  ├─ Cross-team sync meeting
│  ├─ Review JIRA blockers dashboard
│  ├─ Discuss dependencies and timeline
│  └─ Update shared roadmap
│
└─ Sprint boundary:
   ├─ Demo all work (even incomplete dependencies)
   ├─ Identify integration issues early
   └─ Plan for integration testing next sprint

JIRA automation rules:
├─ If Frontend PR approved and Backend API deployed:
│  Then: Create integration test task
│
├─ If Backend endpoint not merged by sprint end:
│  Then: Send escalation to leads
│
└─ If blocker exists > 3 days:
   Then: Notify all watchers (force resolution)

Real example:
"In one project, frontend was blocked by API for 5 days.
By defining API spec first, coordinating on JIRA links,
and using blocking relationships, we reduced wait time
to 1 day. Visible dependencies prevented surprises."

Metrics to track:
├─ Cycle time per team: Should be similar (good coordination)
├─ Blocker duration: Should be < 2 days
├─ Integration issues: Should be minimal (good specs)
└─ Rework: Should be minimal (good upfront alignment)

Key success factors:
✓ API spec defined and approved FIRST
✓ Dependencies explicitly linked in JIRA
✓ Clear ownership and responsibility
✓ Regular cross-team communication
✓ Visible blockers (no hidden problems)
✓ Synchronization points before integration
```

**Why this demonstrates competence:**
- Shows you can handle complex coordination
- Understands JIRA's linking and blocking features
- Thinks about communication and transparency
- Considers both process and technical aspects
- Provides metrics to validate success

