## Interview Questions and Answers

### Beginner Level Questions

**Q1: What is JIRA and what problems does it solve?**

A: JIRA is an issue and project tracking tool developed by Atlassian. It solves several key problems:

1. **Scattered Information**: Without JIRA, team communication happens in emails, Slack, meetings. Information gets lost.
2. **No Progress Visibility**: No central place to see what's being worked on, what's blocked, what's done.
3. **Unclear Priorities**: When everything is important, nothing is. JIRA provides a single backlog.
4. **Accountability**: Who's working on what? When will it be done? JIRA makes this transparent.
5. **Metrics and Improvement**: Without tracking, you can't measure velocity, cycle time, or improve.

JIRA provides a single source of truth for all work.

---

**Q2: Explain the difference between Scrum and Kanban in JIRA.**

A: 
```
Scrum (sprints):
├─ Fixed time period (2-4 weeks)
├─ Team commits to work in sprint planning
├─ Burndown chart tracks progress
├─ Velocity measures team capacity
├─ Best for: Predictable delivery, teams needing structure

Kanban (continuous flow):
├─ No fixed sprints
├─ WIP (Work in Progress) limits control flow
├─ Lead time measures delivery speed
├─ Pull work when capacity available
├─ Best for: Support, maintenance, unpredictable work

In JIRA:
├─ Scrum project: Has sprints, sprint planning, burndown charts
├─ Kanban project: Has WIP limits, continuous flow, cycle time
└─ You pick which fits your team's needs
```

---

**Q3: What is a story point and why not use hours?**

A: Story points are relative estimates of effort/complexity, not absolute hours.

Why story points > hours:
```
Hours problems:
├─ Optimism bias (I think 4 hours, really takes 8)
├─ Interruptions not accounted for
├─ Different people take different time
├─ Accuracy degrades over time
└─ Creates pressure to estimate accurately

Story points benefits:
├─ Relative easier (this is harder than that)
├─ Team consensus (discuss and agree)
├─ Accounts for unknowns naturally
├─ Velocity becomes predictor of team capacity
└─ Removes pressure of "exact" estimates

Example:
"How long is this story?" (Bad answer: "4 hours")
"Is this harder or easier than story X?" (Good approach)
If both team members say "Same complexity", give same points
```

---

**Q4: What are the different issue types in JIRA?**

A:
```
Epic: Large feature (month+ of work)
│ └─ Example: "User Authentication System"

Story: User-facing feature (what user wants)
│ └─ Example: "As a user, I want to reset password"

Task: Technical work (what needs doing)
│ └─ Example: "Upgrade Spring Boot version"

Bug: Something broken
│ └─ Example: "Login fails on mobile Safari"

Sub-task: Part of parent issue
│ └─ Example: Parent=Story, Sub-task=Code, Test, Review

Improvement: Enhancement to existing feature
│ └─ Example: "Make search 2x faster"

Key distinction:
├─ Stories describe WHAT from user perspective
├─ Tasks describe HOW from technical perspective
├─ Bugs describe BROKEN things
└─ Epics organize large bodies of work
```

---

**Q5: What is a Definition of Done?**

A: Definition of Done is a checklist that determines when an issue is truly complete.

```
Example DoD for a story:
☐ Code written and committed
☐ Unit tests written (>80% coverage)
☐ Code reviewed and approved
☐ Integrated with other components
☐ Tested by QA (all acceptance criteria pass)
☐ Documentation updated
☐ Deployed to staging environment
☐ Product owner sign-off obtained

Why it matters:
├─ Prevents "done" meaning different things
├─ Ensures quality standards
├─ Prevents rework later
├─ Team moves on only when truly done
├─ Burndown chart accuracy (done means ACTUALLY done)

If Definition of Done not clear:
├─ Team ships incomplete work
├─ Quality suffers
├─ Rework compounds
└─ Velocity becomes meaningless
```

---

**Q6: What is velocity and why does it matter?**

A:
```
Velocity = Sum of story points completed in sprint

Example:
Sprint 1: Completed 20 points → Velocity = 20
Sprint 2: Completed 21 points → Velocity = 21
Sprint 3: Completed 19 points → Velocity = 19
Average velocity = 20 points per sprint

Why it matters:
├─ Prediction: "At 20 pts/sprint, feature will be done in 3 sprints"
├─ Capacity: "Don't commit more than 20-22 points"
├─ Health: "Velocity dropping = something's wrong"
├─ Morale: "Team achieving committed goals = confidence"
├─ Improvement: "Compare velocities, see if process is better"

Velocity enables sustainable pace:
├─ Overcommit (30 pts, complete 20) = Low morale
├─ Accurate (20 pts, complete 20) = Team feels good
├─ Undercommit (15 pts, complete 20) = False confidence
└─ Consistent accuracy = Team trusts planning
```

