**Q10: What metrics would you track to assess team health?**

A:
```
Key metrics to track:

1. Velocity (stability)
   ├─ Track: Last 6 sprints of story points completed
   ├─ Healthy: Variance < 30% (e.g., 18-22 range)
   ├─ Bad: Variance > 50% (all over place, unpredictable)
   ├─ Use: Predict sprint capacity
   └─ Action: If high variance, find root cause

2. Cycle time (speed)
   ├─ Track: Days from story created to story done
   ├─ Healthy: Decreasing or stable (e.g., 8 days)
   ├─ Bad: Increasing (bottleneck emerging)
   ├─ Use: Measure delivery speed
   └─ Action: If increasing, identify bottleneck

3. Burndown accuracy (predictability)
   ├─ Track: Actual completed vs committed
   ├─ Healthy: >90% commitment accuracy
   ├─ Bad: <80% (missing targets regularly)
   ├─ Use: Build trust in planning
   └─ Action: If low, reassess estimation process

4. Code review turnaround (quality)
   ├─ Track: Hours from PR creation to first review
   ├─ Healthy: <4 hours (same day)
   ├─ Bad: >24 hours (reviews backing up)
   ├─ Use: Quality gate effectiveness
   └─ Action: If slow, add code review to DoD

5. Blocker duration (responsiveness)
   ├─ Track: Days issue stays "Blocked"
   ├─ Healthy: <1 day (fast unblocking)
   ├─ Bad: >3 days (people stuck)
   ├─ Use: Team coordination effectiveness
   └─ Action: If slow, improve escalation path

6. Bug escape rate (quality)
   ├─ Track: Bugs found by QA vs bugs in production
   ├─ Healthy: >90% caught by QA
   ├─ Bad: <80% (customer finding bugs)
   ├─ Use: Testing effectiveness
   └─ Action: If bad, improve QA process

Red flags:
├─ Velocity variance > 50% = Process unstable
├─ Cycle time increasing = Work getting slower
├─ Burndown < 80% = Over-committing
├─ Blockers > 2 days = Dependency management issue
├─ Code review > 8 hours = Resource bottleneck
└─ Bug escape > 20% = Quality issue
```

