### Scenario-Based Questions

**Q15: Your sprint is 80% done and you realize you'll finish 30% over capacity. What happened and what do you do?**

A:
```
Root cause analysis:

Possibilities:
1. Bad estimation (estimated 3, actually 1 point tasks)
   └─ Scope was smaller than thought
   
2. Team velocity higher than historical (estimated 20, will do 26)
   └─ Team got faster (unlikely)
   
3. Scope creep (added 6 points mid-sprint)
   └─ New issues added during sprint
   
4. Burndown lag (work done but not updated)
   └─ Stories not marked done
   
5. Combination of above

Most likely: Combination

Immediate action (end of sprint):

1. Verify accuracy
   ├─ Check: Are stories really close to done?
   ├─ Check: Can they be done by sprint end?
   ├─ Check: What's in-progress vs actually done?
   └─ Update JIRA to reflect reality

2. Finish/push decisions
   ├─ If story finishes by EOD: Mark done
   ├─ If story almost done: Can finalize early next sprint?
   ├─ If story will take days: Move to next sprint now
   └─ Sprint board = actual status

3. Analyze what went well
   ├─ "Why are we delivering 26 instead of 20?"
   ├─ Better estimation learning: Capacity is 26?
   ├─ Team efficiency up: Celebrate
   ├─ Technical debt paid down: Yay
   └─ Use this for future planning

4. Update planning model
   ├─ Was velocity really 20 or was it 24-26?
   ├─ Next sprint: Commit 24 points?
   ├─ Or commit 20 and see if it's again 26?
   ├─ Take data point, don't overreact
   └─ 1-2 anomalies may be flukes

Scenario outcomes:

Best case: Stories actually done, team delivered 26 points
├─ Celebrate: Team exceeded expectations
├─ Learn: Estimation improving or team getting faster
├─ Plan: Next sprint commit 22-23 (build confidence)
└─ Result: Positive energy going forward

Typical case: Stories mostly done, some cleanup
├─ Move: 3 points to next sprint (almost done)
├─ Finish: 23 points (over original 20)
├─ Analysis: Estimation and scope control improvements needed
└─ Plan: Next sprint, tighter scope management

Bad case: Stories not really done, just claimed
├─ Investigate: Why not marked done?
├─ Likely: Definition of Done not clear
├─ Action: Tighten DoD, verify completion
├─ Plan: More careful "done" assessments next sprint

Communication:

"Sprint 23 is finishing with 26 points (vs 20 committed).
This is great! Let's understand why:
├─ Better estimation? (Likely)
├─ Team efficiency? (Maybe)
├─ Scope creep? (Unlikely, we tracked it)

Let's use this data for next sprint planning:
├─ Commit 22-23 points with confidence
├─ See if we continue to deliver 24-26
├─ Three sprints of data = know our real velocity

Team: Great work! This confidence is awesome."
```

